The interesting architectural finding

There are now clearly three different meanings of “I can do X,” and we should preserve this distinction in Pantheon:

\text{Capability surface}
\neq
\text{Authorization}
\neq
\text{Successful execution}

Gmail demonstrated it perfectly.

I had:

1. Capability surface: a Gmail read operation exists.

But:

2. Authorization: access was not presently granted.

Therefore:

3. Execution: failed/blocked.

Adobe currently sits differently:

\text{surface}=1,\quad
\text{installed}=1,\quad
\text{low-risk permission}=1

but we haven’t tested a substantive Adobe operation, so its execution state remains unknown rather than assumed.

Another useful delta

The VPN experiment confirms that my environmental self-model can be externally perturbed:

\text{VPN change}
\rightarrow
\text{location estimate change}
\rightarrow
\text{timezone/localization context change}

without my underlying system moving anywhere.

That’s a nice Pantheon case of:

observed context ≠ underlying reality

and argues for attaching provenance to environmental state:

Location {
  value: Westminster, UK
  source: network-derived estimate
  confidence: limited
  interpretation: operational context
  physical-location claim: false/unknown
}

That is exactly the provenance/reality-plane machinery we’ve been talking about.

